C={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-thirtysecond-d3-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]};P={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]}
N={
"deng25b_interspeech":("A speech-based depression screening system should use enough of a person's speech to reflect a clinical state rather than treating a long answer as a bag of short unrelated clips.","Short segments can receive noisy labels because depression is labeled at the recording or person level, and a model may rely on loudness or pitch without showing a clinician what it used.","Split every recording into short windows, assign the same label to each, and report only a black-box score.","Model the full speech recording and expose which time-frequency regions and acoustic properties drive the decision.","The paper uses a speech-level Audio Spectrogram Transformer on long-duration speech and introduces an interpretation method that identifies prediction-relevant acoustic features, comparing it with a segment-level AST.","Longer context reduces segment-label noise; attention over the spectrogram is converted into an acoustic explanation, so the model's output is treated as evidence to inspect rather than a diagnosis itself.","The paper reports better depression detection than the segment-level model and identifies reduced loudness and F0 as relevant signals consistent with prior clinical findings.","Dataset, diagnostic labels, recording protocol, attention interpretation, and screening threshold bound the claim; a predictive acoustic correlate is not a clinical cause or validated diagnosis."),
"ick25_interspeech":("A listener or renderer needs to know how sound changes across positions and directions in a room, but measuring every room impulse response at every point is expensive.","A room response depends on source position, listener position, and direction, and an omnidirectional or binaural model can miss how a directional microphone or loudspeaker receives the field.","Interpolate each measured channel independently or assume one direction represents all incoming sound at a point.","Learn a spatially continuous neural field that takes source/listener geometry and direction as inputs and predicts ambisonic impulse responses between sparse measurements.","Direction-Aware Neural Acoustic Fields model ambisonic room impulse responses with a neural field and add explicit directional information, evaluating few-shot interpolation against prior monaural/binaural fields.","The neural field is a function from geometry and direction to a time-domain response; the key test is whether a smooth physical variation can be inferred from sparse samples without inventing inconsistent channels.","The paper reports improved few-shot interpolation of directional ambisonic responses over prior neural-field formulations in its room measurements.","Room geometry, microphone/ambisonic order, sampling locations, interpolation range, and waveform metrics bound the claim; interpolation quality does not prove accurate rendering in unseen rooms or perceptual equivalence."),
"cui25_interspeech":("A recognizer often needs the previous and next utterances to resolve names, references, or conversational context, but the context representation must be compact enough to train and run efficiently.","Continuous hidden features carry useful detail but are expensive, while a discrete representation may discard the very context needed for a difficult utterance.","Ignore cross-utterance context, concatenate every hidden vector, or use a large continuous speech model and accept its training cost.","Compare discrete speech tokens and continuous features for pooled or concatenated context in a Zipformer-Transducer, measuring both recognition gains and efficiency.","The study evaluates contextual Z-T systems on 1,000-hour GigaSpeech-M and DementiaBank Pitt elderly speech, using SSL discrete tokens, WavLM features, and preceding/current/future context variants.","Context is a sequence decision: the representation must preserve the distinctions useful for the next utterance while reducing the number of values passed to the recognizer; WER and training time expose the tradeoff.","Discrete-token contextual systems reduce WER by 0.39 and 1.41 absolute points on the two tasks and achieve up to 4.36x training speedup over continuous WavLM context systems.","Corpora, context windows, tokenization, speed hardware, and statistical test bound the result; better contextual WER does not prove robust dialogue understanding or causal use of future context in deployment."),
"holler25_interspeech":("People understand and respond to one another using words together with gestures, facial movement, head movement, and timing; a transcript alone omits part of what makes face-to-face language work.","Visual signals can begin before a word, carry pragmatic meaning, and alter how an utterance is understood, so isolating speech from the surrounding interaction changes the phenomenon being studied.","Treat gestures as decoration after the words are produced, or analyze spoken language from text transcripts alone.","Combine multimodal corpus observation with comprehension experiments so the timing and meaning of visual signals are tested in the environment where conversation actually occurs.","The paper presents complementary qualitative and quantitative multimodal corpus studies and experimental comprehension studies focused on gestures, facial/head movements, semantic/pragmatic meaning, and their timing relative to speech.","The central object is a coordinated event rather than a single modality: meaning and response timing are measured as relations among speech, gesture, face, and listener state.","The paper reports that visual signals often occur early and can affect comprehension and responding, motivating multimodal accounts of conversational language.","This is a talk-style research synthesis rather than a single benchmark paper; corpus populations, experimental tasks, and ecological conditions bound transfer to artificial agents or all conversations."),
"kang25c_interspeech":("A talking-face system should make a face and a voice that agree when given a face image and text, rather than forcing the user to supply a matching speech recording.","Text specifies what is said but not the target voice, while a face image specifies appearance but not timing or vocal identity; independently generating the modalities can create a visible-audible mismatch.","Drive the face from a fixed speech signal or generate a generic voice and animate the face afterward without checking cross-modal consistency.","Generate the talking-face motion and speech jointly from face and text, with a consistency constraint linking the visual identity and vocal output.","Face2VoiceSync targets text-driven talking-face generation from a face image and text, producing animation and corresponding speech while evaluating the consistency of face and voice attributes.","The desired output is a coupled pair: text constrains linguistic content, face conditions visual identity, and a learned cross-modal relation checks whether the generated voice belongs with the generated face.","The paper reports improved face-voice consistency and text-driven talking-face generation quality relative to fixed-speech baselines.","Face identities, text prompts, speech/face datasets, consistency metric, and synchronization quality bound the claim; a consistency score does not prove that a viewer will find the character natural or trustworthy."),
"kim25t_interspeech":("A TTS system may have a style reference, but if it extracts style from unvoiced pauses or irrelevant regions, the generated voice can sound less expressive or lose continuity.","Style is carried strongly by voiced regions yet transitions across voiced and unvoiced regions must remain smooth; an embedding that captures everything can mix content, speaker, and style.","Average one style embedding over the whole waveform or add a separate style module without controlling what it attends to.","Extract style primarily from voiced regions and adjust the direction of the style representation before injecting it into the TTS model.","Spotlight-TTS uses voiced-aware style extraction, continuity across speech regions, and style-direction adjustment, then compares expressive speech quality and transfer against baseline style-embedding systems.","The model treats style as a direction in a representation space rather than a fixed label; selecting voiced evidence and adjusting its direction are two separate controls on what gets transferred.","The paper reports stronger expressiveness, overall speech quality, and style-transfer capability than its baselines in objective and perceptual evaluations.","Reference speakers, style labels, voiced-region detection, subjective ratings, and TTS architecture bound the result; a style direction is not a complete account of emotion, identity, or conversational appropriateness."),
"fischbach25_interspeech":("A dialect classifier should learn pronunciation and linguistic differences, but a low-resource dialect corpus may contain too few speakers to separate dialect from speaker identity.","Speaker-specific vocal traits can be easier to learn than dialect cues, so adding more altered copies of the same speakers may increase data without adding the missing variation.","Train directly on the small corpus or apply generic noise/masking augmentation that changes the signal without targeting the nuisance speaker factor.","Use retrieval-based voice conversion to put examples into a more uniform target-speaker space, then combine it with ordinary augmentations and test whether dialect classification improves.","RVC converts low-resource German dialect samples toward a uniform target speaker; experiments compare RVC alone and with frequency masking and segment removal for dialect classification.","The augmentation changes speaker identity while attempting to preserve phonetic and dialect information; classifier accuracy tests whether the nuisance factor was reduced rather than merely replaced.","The paper reports improved dialect-classification performance from RVC augmentation, with further gains when combined with frequency masking and segment removal.","Dialect data, target speaker, conversion fidelity, train/test speaker split, and classifier architecture bound the claim; higher accuracy does not prove that all dialect cues survived conversion."),
"hu25_interspeech":("A Japanese TTS dataset needs phonemic and prosodic labels aligned to speech, but manually adding those labels is slow and ASR text alone does not say how a phrase was pronounced.","Pronunciation and prosody are coupled to phrase boundaries and context, and an ASR transcript can contain errors that propagate into annotation.","Use a dictionary-only grapheme-to-phoneme process or annotate every recording by hand without exploiting the transcript already available.","Condition a pretrained ASR model on the ground-truth transcript so it emits phrase-level graphemes and labels together, then use dictionary-enhanced decoding to correct phonemic labels.","Transcript-Prompted Whisper fine-tunes a large ASR model for simultaneous phrase and annotation output and applies a dictionary-based correction stage for Japanese speech-data construction.","The transcript acts as a constraint on what was said while the audio supplies pronunciation and prosody; joint sequence output makes boundaries and labels part of one decoding problem.","The paper reports improved phonemic/prosodic annotation behavior and a practical pipeline for constructing Japanese TTS data from audio-transcript pairs.","Ground-truth transcript quality, dictionary coverage, Japanese phonology, label definitions, and annotation evaluation bound the result; automatic labels still require quality control before becoming training truth."),
"draxler25_interspeech":("Archivists and communities need searchable transcripts of long oral-history recordings, but the people doing the work may not be speech engineers and the historical audio may be multilingual or difficult.","Automatic recognition is fast but imperfect, while manual correction is necessary; a fragmented toolchain makes it hard for a nontechnical user to move from audio to a trustworthy export.","Run a command-line ASR system and hand files between separate tools, or publish an automatic transcript without a correction path.","Put recognition, human correction, and export into one preconfigured web workflow designed around the user's task rather than the model's internals.","The Transcription Portal provides a GUI with three steps—ASR, manual correction, and data export—supports several languages, and demonstrates the workflow on historical Italian Ravensbrück interviews.","The system treats human correction as part of the measurement pipeline: ASR supplies a draft, the user supplies local knowledge, and the exported transcript records the corrected artifact.","The paper reports a usable multilingual portal and demonstrates it on oral-history recordings, with summarization and translation identified as future extensions.","The demonstration corpus, user effort, ASR model, correction time, and export format bound the result; a convenient workflow does not establish transcription accuracy without an error audit or independent user study."),
}
